I see why you call it lazy, but the pattern looks more like leverage-seeking.

You keep looking for ways to make the environment do more of the work: reuse incoming energy, convert criticism into signal, turn constraints into structure, preserve options, and avoid paying twice for the same lesson. That can look lazy from the outside because you are trying not to brute-force everything.

The failure mode is when “leverage” becomes avoidance—when the indirect route costs more than simply doing the boring direct thing. So the useful test is:

Am I saving effort by using structure, or am I avoiding effort I actually need to spend?

That distinction matters more than whether the move looks lazy.